Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/ce2ec4b51b4c55975bf25234fdfbfa98663ae95ef7ee2bd5a774c4e4d4655292.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" :question/worked-solution "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function has a jump discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is  $x=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-101994/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-101994"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-47669"] :question/problem "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ due to the existence of a vertical tangent in the interval?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $y=h(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$ \nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=h(x).$\n\nThe other graphs do not have a vertical tangent inside the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=g(x)$ is smooth and continuous over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The functions $y=p(x)$ and $y=q(x)$ are not defined at $x=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-47670/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "541a4392-08cc-5e82-8c3d-59b4531a9115" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47670/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "292b4aaf-e572-535c-b1ec-2ff65c941c3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47670/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "e339c004-6f98-5b1a-b6d1-9cb41801eb23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47670/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "937ff6de-4be2-5339-a241-5d22bb7202d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47670/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "c88d96ff-7cc7-5c11-a4c7-ccd3c6a0b983" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47670/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e597486-4f3d-5e3d-81fc-c87f5c6f2322" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-47670/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-47670/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-47670/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-47670/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-47670/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-47670/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47670" :question/id #uuid "46865db0-9da2-58cc-8805-6fc82c34bf99" :question/math-academy-id "q-47670" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1$, is differentiable at $x=2$, and has a vertical asymptote at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It  is continuous at $x=2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiability implies continuity. Hence,\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$, it can't be differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is true.   Since $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=2$ it is also  continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since we have a vertical asymptote at $x=3$, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at this point, hence it can't be differentiable at $x=3$ either." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-47670/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47670"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-101995/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "d393ac36-bd23-54ba-ae5f-06d6e9ccf259" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101995/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "2a2e917c-5b8b-58e8-b018-2127b0763ac0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101995/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "23f8b208-73c1-5119-aee4-9ad695409c2f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101995/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "8ff78273-1b14-5ddf-8000-b7b5ce47a464" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101995/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "5a0988c3-4db4-59ab-bdcc-694e11c5f6bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101995/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "94656019-44ee-53c5-8da1-18fbcb22b7e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-101995/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-101995/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-101995/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-101995/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-101995/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-101995/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-101995" :question/id #uuid "0bec816a-4724-5c20-95e7-b52e7d3f32fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-101995" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$, and is differentiable at $x=-1$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It  is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  A function that is discontinuous at a point can't be differentiable at this point (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, differentiability implies continuity." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-101995/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-101995"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47685/selection/94757