nswer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-47685/selection/81ace704fcbc9261043bf9d8365bc555ebbfd46fd206cfdca7b875ca546c2616" :answer-field/choices ["q-47685/selection/9475792d702900b5ed810c1ad3554966518ed7b0d36f51277dd0285a4e8fd863" "q-47685/selection/6be3422a3e9cb5d1c8faf48fee8166259f5c3b6ecdb95a67ae67e5300a9ff0d0" "q-47685/selection/81ace704fcbc9261043bf9d8365bc555ebbfd46fd206cfdca7b875ca546c2616" "q-47685/selection/7c29cf7e7dbc7b868d0516e2d81695d4463aac41f49ac9d03e1740e4277772a7" "q-47685/selection/342e12447a6c6b78d3b8cdc1837b06966fe8119431b0066073ca22c731a18185"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47685" :question/id #uuid "0ccce8a3-bf9d-5a78-aab6-47b5f04c990b" :question/math-academy-id "q-47685" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ due to the existence of a cusp?" :question/worked-solution "The function $y=q(x)$ has a cusp at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$ So $y=q(x)$ is the correct answer.\n\nThe other graphs do not have a cusp within the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=g(x)$ is smooth and continuous over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ and is therefore differentiable at every point in the interval.\n\n- The function $y=p(x)$ is not defined at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-47685/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47685"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-73267/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "5bd0f9c3-2d18-57a4-92b9-691a28014f86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73267/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "52d638f2-0a10-50f1-9c4a-defa261ce77c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73267/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "19881840-23cb-5bd9-af51-c86a13510dfe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73267/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "8b1d8d21-9a86-5967-ac82-24017082b5f8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73267/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "cdf8a5e6-9613-5a14-844b-a46eb9ce6338" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73267/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e1287e4-30db-5043-afeb-beaf61b74880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-73267/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-73267/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-73267/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-73267/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-73267/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-73267/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-73267" :question/id #uuid "68d793ec-aaf3-5b37-877d-af06a0a967e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73267" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-1, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 1, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is **not** continuous at $x=0$ but it is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement I is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(0)=1,\n$$\n\n \nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ Therefore, it's not differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-73267/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-73267"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47681/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "b75398cb-dcef-57d8-a605-625b7e970d42" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47681/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "536cfb33-9ec5-5e57-9014-9be7d8a50778" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47681/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "ae34c201-b60c-5ae3-8ebf-150bc26bd7d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47681/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "b00ef24b-b0fa-5085-ae3a-db8fea58916d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47681/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "c24e39f8-84e1-5a75-bf0e-4a9ffd7fa725" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47681/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b9c7b53b-b0d7-5232-a529-b0fbb684c697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-47681/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-47681/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-47681/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-47681/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-47681/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-47681/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47681" :question/id #uuid "f0ff0842-96b0-56ec-a561-8c9fa764c1ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-47681" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+1, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 3, & x=1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(-1,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually. \n\n- Statement I is false.  At $x=1$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=3,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ ($f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. Since  $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-47681/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47681"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-102159/selection/7febb6474be0a96f7a813deea10dcb9a3fa4f8c81d683c3c25667331c0c59eea" :answer/id #uuid "a9ed2876-9c70-5df1-bea3-28a1d84eda17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & x<-1 \\\\ x-1,\\, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102159/selection/f08b64adc6c8a957ea0d098247ced1ae6210a73d2c4c82f538e0ec5340e42bd0" :answer/id #uuid "f2845557-be1e-5715-a89d-8f255fcd1de1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p(x)=\\sqrt[5]{x+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102159/selection/873d64b7aa60e0d4f3782d24351209c967ebf8f4da99cc008244f65ca1afc210" :answer/id #uuid "18477b97-c937-5785-977f-8313442363c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102159/selection/bb52f5732385c365c4d09041f2d3ea72f261af4bb56501a4e297a287fba0f32c" :answer/id #uuid "a054dab7-6cfb-5117-9710-009355c0a7b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102159/selection/e564cc3d41f46f89a1397092f77d10106e97a109c4047b75a1862957ef55b3ca" :answer/id #uuid "bcb3904f-ab57-574e-95ba-587ffbff42a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102159/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5d3f4a-4c41-569b-bdf4-aa84204fa7f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-102159/selection/e564cc3d41f46f89a1397092f77d10106e97a109c4047b75a1862957ef55b3ca" :answer-field/choices ["q-102159/selection/7febb6474be0a96f7a813deea10dcb9a3fa4f8c81d683c3c25667331c0c59eea" "q-102159/selection/f08b64adc6c8a957ea0d098247ced1ae6210a73d2c4c82f538e0ec5340e42bd0" "q-102159/selection/873d64b7aa60e0d4f3782d24351209c967ebf8f4da99cc008244f65ca1afc210" "q-102159/selection/bb52f5732385c365c4d09041f2d3ea72f261af4bb56501a4e297a287fba0f32c" "q-102159/selection/e564cc3d41f46f89a1397092f77d10106e97a109c4047b75a1862957ef55b3ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-102159" :question/id #uuid "46edc91d-e7f5-50a1-b76e-fb0eba0a8481" :question/math-academy-id "q-102159" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $h(x)$ is differentiable for all $x\\ne 1.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$\n\nThe other functions are not differentiable at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-102159/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-102159"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47680/selection/a7eb67c42e330c74efee37da2d88d95f09be9b8eeeedba5321a5e191ac87b586" :answer/id #uuid "3f36fdd6-c55a-5179-8a26-414408ac7d34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "g(x)=2|x+2|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47680/selection/aeedc0ebf1d37c81eb53781a554945c79827cebe0e7c7e820356386f6d4a58ca" :answer/id #uuid "36ed0c88-6717-5b68-a863-82a514be1196" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}x,\\, & x<-2 \\\\ x-2,\\, & x\\ge -2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47680/selection/1e6fe1498bb8ccd4ca001c854796e9a0f6c8fc99a012c71772f8536dc7f653c3" :answer/id #uuid "140e4e13-b1ff-5cbb-b34b-6f86109cc2a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x+2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47680/selection/82e4a4c0a49ae39679209e345212a34273af40eefdbe9d0c8a6b041d50757ead" :answer/id #uuid "bd58eda1-413f-5637-8d17-0bbb88b3f8c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47680/selection/cc9b189905709f8049ec86c89025144ea23de77656e634ea4cc94b17a9d6af8d" :answer/id #uuid "b73f7d30-ca81-5401-b313-fccb31b50d2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47680/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "453aab19-db93-56bf-bd0d-77c5046deb7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-47680/selection/cc9b189905709f8049ec86c89025144ea23de77656e634ea4cc94b17a9d6af8d" :answer-field/choices ["q-47680/selection/a7eb67c42e330c74efee37da2d88d95f09be9b8eeeedba5321a5e191ac87b586" "q-47680/selection/aeedc0ebf1d37c81eb53781a554945c79827cebe0e7c7e820356386f6d4a58ca" "q-47680/selection/1e6fe1498bb8ccd4ca001c854796e9a0f6c8fc99a012c71772f8536dc7f653c3" "q-47680/selection/82e4a4c0a49ae39679209e345212a34273af40eefdbe9d0c8a6b041d50757ead" "q-47680/selection/cc9b189905709f8049ec86c89025144ea23de77656e634ea4cc94b17a9d6af8d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47680" :question/id #uuid "7d0eb31e-00b8-5cab-baf1-ea4a71c1aca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47680" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $p(x)$ is differentiable for all real number $x.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $p(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nAll other functions are not differentiable at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=-2$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-2$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $h(x)$has a vertical tangent at $x=-2$, and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2$ and hence it is not differentiable there." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-47680/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47680"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-73270/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "328d4ec9-fc9a-58ef-a77f-873385df118f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73270/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "3a4a061c-1dce-539c-bb12-aaa3ed87a30f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73270/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "9408aea0-188c-576f-9cbc-30b4f7d7dcb1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73270/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "bdbfa959-c4b3-57a8-8635-ab5704290f17" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73270/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b971f998-7ede-5e2a-997e-b66429b4ce86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73270/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "86d977f7-1583-58e8-898d-6a3d5a2b8050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-73270/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-73270/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-73270/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-73270/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-73270/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-73270/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-73270" :question/id #uuid "9d3d55e7-45c3-56ac-8489-9d0883fda9fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-73270" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x<1 \\\\ 2, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I and II are true. Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly at  $x=1.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x+1)=1+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is  continuous at $x=1$ . However, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=1\\,$  if  $\\,x<1$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>1$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-73270/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-73270"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-131"] :question/problem "Is the function $y=f(x),$ plotted below, differentiable at $x=-1?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/fafd2847f21de3f9f35e05dac61e8a5e406ede40ef7fba014ca5c01eb6d6cdd6.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function $y=f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=-1.$\n\nThe function has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Since it is not continuous at $x=-1,$ it is also not differentiable there." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-133"] :question/problem "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is plotted below. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/59499238b529d4d583885cf2c06bae11991c7d3fb5f84f347158e774e2b4ae00.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a vertical tangent, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=2$ the function has a removable discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0, 1, 2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-132"] :question/problem "Which of the following functions are differentiable at the point $x=-1?$\n\n- $f(x) = x^5+1$\n\n- $g(x) = \\dfrac{2}{x+1}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is a polynomial and is therefore differentiable and continuous for all $x.$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=-1,$ so it is not differentiable at $x=-1$ either.\n\nIn conclusion, only $f(x)$ is differentiable at the point $x=-1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
